From: Richard Fritz <gh@chaos4all.de>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:37:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] ipa: ipu3: Add lens shading correction

The ImgU has a shading correction (SHD) block that multiplies each Bayer
pixel by a per-channel gain, bilinearly interpolated from a grid of gain
values. The IPU3 IPA doesn't configure it, leaving the kernel defaults
(unity gains) in place, so images from sensors with strong lens shading
show vignetting and colour casts.

Add an Lsc algorithm that programs the SHD block from tuning data. The
tuning data describes the shading gains on a uniform grid of sample
points spanning the full sensor pixel array, for one or more colour
temperatures:

  - Lsc:
      grid: [ 33, 25 ]
      sets:
        - ct: 5000
          r: [ ... ]
          gr: [ ... ]
          gb: [ ... ]
          b: [ ... ]

At configure time, the algorithm selects the finest hardware grid that
covers the BDS output frame within the hardware limits (at most 73 nodes
per line, 28 LUT sets of 146 cells, power-of-two block sizes), maps each
grid node through the BDS scaling, the input feeder crop and the sensor
analogue crop and binning to the pixel array, and resamples the tuning
tables bilinearly. The channels are named after the pixel colour, as the
kernel realigns the input frame to the GRBG order used internally by the
ImgU.

At runtime, the tables are interpolated by the colour temperature
estimated by the AWB algorithm, converted to the hardware format and
written to the parameters buffer for every frame. The UAPI doesn't
document the LUT format beyond the gain ranges selected by the gain
factor. The algorithm assumes 12-bit values with 10 fractional bits
added to an implicit unity gain, and raises the gain factor when gains
exceed 5.0. The LensShadingCorrectionEnable control allows disabling
the correction at runtime.

When no Lsc entry is present in the tuning file, the IPA behaves as
before.

Tested with an OV8858 on an HP Pro x2 612 G2, with tables calibrated
from flat-field captures. Together with the correct black level for the
sensor, added in subsequent patches, the corner to centre ratio of R/G
on a flat field goes from 0.62-0.66 to 1.02-1.03.

